JavaScript Reference
This document covers the JavaScript features used throughout this course. The course uses a constrained subset of the language to keep examples consistent and readable across all units. Read through this before starting the units and return to it when you need a reminder.

Two rules apply everywhere in the course:

Functions are always defined as arrow functions: const fn = () => ...
Classes are never used; objects are plain literals with arrow function methods
Variables
Use const for values that will not be reassigned. Use let for values that will change. Avoid var.

const port = 3000;
const name = "Alice";

let count = 0;
count = count + 1;
Reassigning a const is a runtime error. For objects and arrays, const prevents reassignment of the variable itself, not mutation of its contents.

const config = { port: 3000 };
config.port = 4000; // allowed — mutating the object
config = {}; // TypeError — reassigning the variable
MDN: constLinks to an external site. | MDN: letLinks to an external site.

Data Types
JavaScript has six primitive types and two compound types.

Primitives:

string — text: 'hello', "world", `template`
number — integers and floats: 42, 3.14, NaN, Infinity
boolean — true or false
null — explicit absence of a value
undefined — a variable declared but not yet assigned
symbol — unique identifiers (rarely used in this course)
Compound:

object — key/value pairs
array — ordered list (technically also an object)
Use typeof to inspect a type at runtime:

typeof "hello"; // 'string'
typeof 42; // 'number'
typeof true; // 'boolean'
typeof null; // 'object'  — a known JS quirk
typeof undefined; // 'undefined'
typeof {}; // 'object'
typeof []; // 'object'
Array.isArray([]); // true
MDN: JavaScript data typesLinks to an external site.

Strings
String literals use single quotes, double quotes, or backticks. Backtick strings (template literals) support embedded expressions and multi-line text.

const greeting = "Hello";
const name = "world";
const message = `${greeting}, ${name}!`; // 'Hello, world!'

const multiline = `
  Line one
  Line two
`;
Common string methods:

const s = "  Hello, World!  ";

s.length; // 17
s.trim(); // 'Hello, World!'
s.toLowerCase(); // '  hello, world!  '
s.toUpperCase(); // '  HELLO, WORLD!  '
s.includes("World"); // true
s.startsWith("  Hello"); // true
s.endsWith("!  "); // true
s.indexOf("World"); // 9
s.slice(2, 7); // 'Hello'
s.replace("World", "Alice"); // '  Hello, Alice!  '
s.split(", "); // ['  Hello', 'World!  ']
"abc".repeat(3); // 'abcabcabc'
"5".padStart(3, "0"); // '005'
MDN: StringLinks to an external site. | MDN: Template literalsLinks to an external site.

Numbers
JavaScript uses 64-bit floating-point for all numbers. Integer arithmetic behaves as expected for typical values.

const a = 10;
const b = 3;

a + b; // 13
a - b; // 7
a * b; // 30
a / b; // 3.3333...
a % b; // 1   (remainder)
a ** b; // 1000 (exponentiation)
The Math object provides common utilities:

Math.floor(3.9); // 3
Math.ceil(3.1); // 4
Math.round(3.5); // 4
Math.abs(-5); // 5
Math.min(1, 2, 3); // 1
Math.max(1, 2, 3); // 3
Math.sqrt(16); // 4
Math.pow(2, 10); // 1024
Math.random(); // random float in [0, 1)
Convert between strings and numbers:

Number("42"); // 42
Number("abc"); // NaN
parseInt("42px"); // 42
parseFloat("3.14"); // 3.14
String(42)(
  // '42'
  42,
).toString(); // '42'
Check for special values:

Number.isNaN(NaN); // true
Number.isFinite(Infinity); // false
Number.isInteger(4.0); // true
MDN: NumberLinks to an external site. | MDN: MathLinks to an external site.

Booleans and Comparison
true && false; // false  (logical AND)
true || false; // true   (logical OR)
!true; // false  (logical NOT)
Use === (strict equality) and !== (strict inequality). Avoid == and !=, which coerce types in surprising ways.

1 === 1; // true
1 === "1"; // false  — different types
1 == "1"; // true   — type coercion (avoid this)
Truthy and falsy: false, 0, '', null, undefined, and NaN are falsy. Everything else is truthy.

if (0) {
  /* never runs */
}
if ("") {
  /* never runs */
}
if (null) {
  /* never runs */
}
if ([]) {
  /* runs — empty array is truthy */
}
if ({}) {
  /* runs — empty object is truthy */
}
MDN: Equality comparisonsLinks to an external site. | MDN: Logical operatorsLinks to an external site.

Arrow Functions
Arrow functions are the only function syntax used in this course.

// no arguments
const greet = () => "Hello!";

// one argument — parentheses are optional, but include them for consistency
const double = (n) => n * 2;

// multiple arguments
const add = (a, b) => a + b;

// multi-line body — braces required, return must be explicit
const clamp = (value, min, max) => {
  if (value < min) return min;
  if (value > max) return max;
  return value;
};

// implicit return of an object — wrap in parentheses to avoid ambiguity
const makePoint = (x, y) => ({ x, y });
Default parameters:

const connect = (host = "localhost", port = 3000) => `${host}:${port}`;

connect(); // 'localhost:3000'
connect("example.com", 443); // 'example.com:443'
Async arrow functions:

const fetchData = async (url) => {
  const response = await fetch(url);
  return response.json();
};
Arrow functions do not have their own this binding. In this course that is rarely relevant because we reference objects by name rather than via this.

MDN: Arrow function expressionsLinks to an external site. | MDN: Default parametersLinks to an external site.

Arrays
Arrays are ordered lists. Indices start at 0.

const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"];

fruits[0]; // 'apple'
fruits.length; // 3
fruits[fruits.length - 1]; // 'cherry' (last element)
Mutating methods (modify the array in place):

const a = [1, 2, 3];

a.push(4); // appends 4; returns new length (4)
a.pop(); // removes and returns last element (4)
a.unshift(0); // prepends 0; returns new length
a.shift(); // removes and returns first element (0)
a.splice(1, 1); // removes 1 element at index 1; returns removed elements
a.reverse(); // reverses in place
a.sort(); // sorts in place (lexicographic by default)
Non-mutating methods (return a new value, original unchanged):

const nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6];

nums.slice(2, 5); // [4, 1, 5]  — copy from index 2 up to (not including) 5
nums.concat([7, 8]); // [3,1,4,1,5,9,2,6,7,8]
nums.includes(4); // true
nums.indexOf(1); // 1  — first occurrence
nums
  .join(", ") // '3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6'
  [(1, [2, [3]])].flat() // [1, 2, [3]]   — one level deep
  [(1, [2, [3]])].flat(Infinity); // [1, 2, 3]     — fully flattened
Sorting with a comparator:

const sorted = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5].sort((a, b) => a - b); // [1, 1, 3, 4, 5]
MDN: ArrayLinks to an external site.

Functional Array Methods
These are the primary iteration tools in this course. Each takes an arrow function as its argument and does not modify the original array.

.map()
Transforms every element; returns a new array of the same length.

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];

const doubled = numbers.map((n) => n * 2); // [2, 4, 6, 8]
const labels = numbers.map((n) => `item-${n}`); // ['item-1', 'item-2', ...]
.filter()
Keeps elements for which the callback returns true; returns a new (possibly shorter) array.

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

const evens = numbers.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0); // [2, 4, 6]
const big = numbers.filter((n) => n > 3); // [4, 5, 6]
.reduce()
Accumulates a single value from all elements. The second argument to reduce is the initial accumulator value.

const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];

const sum = numbers.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0); // 10
const max = numbers.reduce((acc, n) => (n > acc ? n : acc), -Infinity); // 4
Building an object from an array:

const words = ["cat", "dog", "cat", "bird", "dog", "dog"];

const counts = words.reduce((acc, word) => {
  acc[word] = (acc[word] ?? 0) + 1;
  return acc;
}, {});
// { cat: 2, dog: 3, bird: 1 }
.find() and .findIndex()
Return the first matching element or its index. Return undefined / -1 if not found.

const users = [
  { id: 1, name: "Alice" },
  { id: 2, name: "Bob" },
];

users.find((u) => u.id === 2); // { id: 2, name: 'Bob' }
users.findIndex((u) => u.id === 2); // 1
.some() and .every()
Return a boolean.

const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];

nums.some((n) => n > 4); // true   — at least one element matches
nums.every((n) => n > 0); // true   — all elements match
nums.every((n) => n > 2); // false
.forEach()
Runs a callback for side effects; returns undefined. Prefer .map() when you need the results.

[1, 2, 3].forEach((n) => console.log(n));
.flatMap()
Maps then flattens one level. Useful when each element produces an array of results.

const sentences = ["hello world", "foo bar"];
const words = sentences.flatMap((s) => s.split(" "));
// ['hello', 'world', 'foo', 'bar']
MDN: Array.prototype.mapLinks to an external site. | MDN: filterLinks to an external site. | MDN: reduceLinks to an external site.

Objects
Objects are key/value pairs. Keys are strings (or symbols); values can be anything.

const server = {
  host: "localhost",
  port: 3000,
  tls: false,
};

server.host; // 'localhost'  — dot notation
server["port"]; // 3000         — bracket notation (useful for dynamic keys)
Shorthand property names: when a variable name matches the key, you can omit the value.

const host = "localhost";
const port = 3000;

const config = { host, port }; // same as { host: host, port: port }
Computed property names:

const key = "status";
const obj = { [key]: 200 }; // { status: 200 }
Methods as arrow function properties: this is how functions are attached to objects in this course.

const counter = {
  value: 0,
  increment: () => {
    counter.value++;
  },
  reset: () => {
    counter.value = 0;
  },
  get: () => counter.value,
};

counter.increment();
counter.get(); // 1
Note: arrow functions do not bind this, so methods reference the object by its variable name rather than via this.

Object utility methods:

const obj = { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 };

Object.keys(obj); // ['a', 'b', 'c']
Object.values(obj); // [1, 2, 3]
Object.entries(obj); // [['a', 1], ['b', 2], ['c', 3]]

Object.fromEntries([
  ["a", 1],
  ["b", 2],
]); // { a: 1, b: 2 }
Object.assign({}, obj, { d: 4 }); // { a: 1, b: 2, c: 3, d: 4 }
Spread operator on objects (shallow copy / merge):

const base = { host: "localhost", port: 3000 };
const override = { port: 8080, tls: true };

const merged = { ...base, ...override };
// { host: 'localhost', port: 8080, tls: true }
MDN: ObjectLinks to an external site. | MDN: Spread syntaxLinks to an external site.

Destructuring
Destructuring extracts values from objects and arrays into named variables.

Object destructuring:

const config = { host: "localhost", port: 3000, tls: false };

const { host, port } = config; // host = 'localhost', port = 3000

// rename while destructuring
const { host: serverHost } = config; // serverHost = 'localhost'

// default values (used when the key is absent)
const { timeout = 5000 } = config; // timeout = 5000
Array destructuring:

const [first, second, ...rest] = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50];
// first = 10, second = 20, rest = [30, 40, 50]

const [, second] = [10, 20, 30]; // skip first element
In function parameters:

const greet = ({ name, greeting = "Hello" }) => `${greeting}, ${name}!`;

greet({ name: "Alice" }); // 'Hello, Alice!'
greet({ name: "Bob", greeting: "Hi" }); // 'Hi, Bob!'
MDN: Destructuring assignmentLinks to an external site.

Spread and Rest
Spread expands an iterable into individual elements. Rest collects remaining elements into an array. Both use ....

// spread an array into function arguments
const nums = [1, 2, 3];
Math.max(...nums); // 3

// copy and extend an array
const a = [1, 2];
const b = [...a, 3, 4]; // [1, 2, 3, 4]

// rest parameters — collect extra arguments
const sum = (first, ...others) => others.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, first);
sum(1, 2, 3, 4); // 10
MDN: Rest parametersLinks to an external site. | MDN: Spread syntaxLinks to an external site.

Control Flow
if / else:

const classify = (n) => {
  if (n > 0) return "positive";
  else if (n < 0) return "negative";
  else return "zero";
};
Ternary operator — for simple conditional expressions:

const label = (n) => (n >= 0 ? "non-negative" : "negative");
switch:

const describe = (status) => {
  switch (status) {
    case 200:
      return "OK";
    case 404:
      return "Not Found";
    case 500:
      return "Server Error";
    default:
      return "Unknown";
  }
};
Short-circuit evaluation:

const name = user.name || "Anonymous"; // use 'Anonymous' if name is falsy
const port = options.port ?? 3000; // use 3000 only if port is null/undefined
user.isAdmin && performAdminTask(); // call only if isAdmin is truthy
MDN: if...elseLinks to an external site. | MDN: Conditional (ternary) operatorLinks to an external site. | MDN: switchLinks to an external site.

Iteration
.map(), .filter(), .reduce() handle synchronous iteration over arrays. See the Functional Array Methods section above.

for...of iterates over any iterable (arrays, strings, Maps, Sets). Use it when you need sequential await inside the loop body.

const urls = ["https://api.example.com/a", "https://api.example.com/b"];

for (const url of urls) {
  const data = await fetch(url); // awaited one at a time, in order
  console.log(data);
}
Iterating object entries:

const scores = { Alice: 95, Bob: 87, Carol: 92 };

for (const [name, score] of Object.entries(scores)) {
  console.log(`${name}: ${score}`);
}
MDN: for...ofLinks to an external site.

Promises
A Promise represents a value that will be available in the future. Most async APIs in Node.js return promises. You will mostly use async/await rather than .then() chains, but several Promise utility functions are useful.

// run multiple promises in parallel; fail fast if any one fails
const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([fetchUser(id), fetchPosts(id)]);

// run multiple promises in parallel; collect all outcomes regardless of failure
const results = await Promise.allSettled([fetchA(), fetchB()]);
results.forEach((r) => {
  if (r.status === "fulfilled") console.log(r.value);
  else console.error(r.reason);
});

// return the result of whichever promise settles first
const fastest = await Promise.race([slowFetch(), fastFetch()]);
Creating a promise manually (useful for wrapping callback-based APIs or for delays):

const delay = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

await delay(1000); // pause for 1 second
MDN: PromiseLinks to an external site. | MDN: Promise.allLinks to an external site. | MDN: Promise.allSettledLinks to an external site.

Async/Await
async and await let you write asynchronous code that reads like synchronous code. An async arrow function always returns a Promise.

const delay = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));

const run = async () => {
  console.log("start");
  await delay(1000);
  console.log("one second later");
  await delay(500);
  console.log("done");
};

run();
Parallel execution with Promise.all:

const fetchUserAndPosts = async (userId) => {
  const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([
    fetchUser(userId),
    fetchPosts(userId),
  ]);
  return { user, posts };
};
Both fetches start at the same time instead of waiting for each other in sequence.

MDN: async function expressionLinks to an external site. | MDN: awaitLinks to an external site.

Error Handling
try / catch:

const parseJSON = (text) => {
  try {
    return JSON.parse(text);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Invalid JSON:", err.message);
    return null;
  }
};
try / catch with async/await:

const loadData = async (url) => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
    return await response.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed to load:", err.message);
    return null;
  }
};
Throwing errors:

const divide = (a, b) => {
  if (b === 0) throw new Error("Cannot divide by zero");
  return a / b;
};
finally — runs whether or not an error was thrown:

const withCleanup = async () => {
  const resource = await acquire();
  try {
    return await use(resource);
  } finally {
    await release(resource);
  }
};
MDN: try...catchLinks to an external site. | MDN: ErrorLinks to an external site.

ES6 Modules
The course uses ES6 modules throughout. Files use .js extensions and package.json must set "type": "module".

Named exports:

// math.js
export const add = (a, b) => a + b;
export const multiply = (a, b) => a * b;
Named imports:

import { add, multiply } from "./math.js";

add(2, 3); // 5
multiply(2, 3); // 6
Default export:

// logger.js
const logger = {
  info: (msg) => console.log(`[INFO] ${msg}`),
  error: (msg) => console.error(`[ERROR] ${msg}`),
};

export default logger;
Default import:

import logger from "./logger.js";

logger.info("Server started");
Re-export:

export { add } from "./math.js";
Note: if you see require() in an example outside this course, it uses CommonJS, which predates ES6 modules. Node.js supports both, but this course uses import/export exclusively.

MDN: exportLinks to an external site. | MDN: importLinks to an external site.

Useful Operators
Optional chaining (?.) — access a property only if the preceding value is not null/undefined:

const user = { profile: { name: "Alice" } };

user.profile?.name; // 'Alice'
user.address?.city; // undefined — no error even though address is absent
user.greet?.(); // undefined — safe method call
Nullish coalescing (??) — return the right side only when the left is null or undefined. Unlike ||, it does not trigger on 0 or ''.

const port = process.env.PORT ?? 3000; // 3000 if PORT is not set

0 || "default"; // 'default'  — 0 is falsy
0 ?? "default"; // 0          — 0 is not null/undefined
Logical assignment:

let a = null;
a ??= "fallback"; // assigns only if null or undefined

let b = 0;
b ||= 42; // assigns if falsy

let c = 5;
c &&= c * 2; // assigns if truthy
MDN: Optional chainingLinks to an external site. | MDN: Nullish coalescingLinks to an external site.

JSON
JSON is the standard data format for APIs and configuration files.

const obj = { name: "Alice", scores: [95, 87, 92] };

const json = JSON.stringify(obj);
// '{"name":"Alice","scores":[95,87,92]}'

const pretty = JSON.stringify(obj, null, 2);
// {
//   "name": "Alice",
//   "scores": [
//     95,
//     87,
//     92
//   ]
// }

const parsed = JSON.parse(json);
// { name: 'Alice', scores: [95, 87, 92] }
JSON.stringify and JSON.parse throw on invalid input, so wrap them in try/catch when handling untrusted data.

MDN: JSONLinks to an external site.

Maps and Sets
Map stores key/value pairs where keys can be any type, not just strings. It preserves insertion order and is more predictable than using a plain object as a dictionary.

const cache = new Map();

cache.set("user:1", { name: "Alice" });
cache.get("user:1"); // { name: 'Alice' }
cache.has("user:1"); // true
cache.size; // 1
cache.delete("user:1");

for (const [key, value] of cache) {
  console.log(key, value);
}
Set stores unique values; duplicates are silently ignored.

const ids = new Set([1, 2, 3, 2, 1]);

ids.size; // 3  — duplicates removed
ids.has(2); // true
ids.add(4);
ids.delete(1);

// convert to array
const unique = [...new Set([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3])]; // [1, 2, 3]
MDN: MapLinks to an external site. | MDN: SetLinks to an external site.

Alternative formats
