+# JavaScript Reference
+
+Source: COMPSCI 426 Canvas page, "JavaScript".
+
+This course uses a constrained JavaScript subset to keep examples consistent
+and readable.
+
+Core rules:
+
+- Define functions as arrow functions: `const fn = () => ...`.
+- Do not use classes; use plain objects with arrow function methods.
+- Use ES6 modules with `import`/`export`; avoid CommonJS `require()`.
+- In `package.json`, set `"type": "module"` for course code.
+
+## Variables
+
+Use `const` for values that will not be reassigned. Use `let` for values that
+change. Avoid `var`.
+
+```js
+const port = 3000;
+const name = "Alice";
+
+let count = 0;
+count = count + 1;
+```
+
+`const` prevents reassignment, not object mutation:
+
+```js
+const config = { port: 3000 };
+config.port = 4000; // allowed
+// config = {}; // TypeError
+```
+
+## Data Types
+
+Primitive types:
+
+- `string`: text, such as `"hello"` or `` `template` ``.
+- `number`: integers, floats, `NaN`, `Infinity`.
+- `boolean`: `true` or `false`.
+- `null`: explicit absence.
+- `undefined`: declared but not assigned.
+- `symbol`: unique identifiers, rarely used in this course.
+
+Compound types:
+
+- `object`: key/value pairs.
+- `array`: ordered list, technically also an object.
+
+```js
+typeof "hello"; // "string"
+typeof 42; // "number"
+typeof true; // "boolean"
+typeof null; // "object", historical JS quirk
+typeof undefined; // "undefined"
+typeof {}; // "object"
+typeof []; // "object"
+Array.isArray([]); // true
+```
+
+## Strings
+
+Strings can use single quotes, double quotes, or backticks. Template literals
+support embedded expressions and multiline text.
+
+```js
+const greeting = "Hello";
+const name = "world";
+const message = `${greeting}, ${name}!`;
+
+const multiline = `
+  Line one
+  Line two
+`;
+```
+
+Common methods:
+
+```js
+const s = "  Hello, World!  ";
+
+s.length;
+s.trim();
+s.toLowerCase();
+s.toUpperCase();
+s.includes("World");
+s.startsWith("  Hello");
+s.endsWith("!  ");
+s.indexOf("World");
+s.slice(2, 7);
+s.replace("World", "Alice");
+s.split(", ");
+"abc".repeat(3);
+"5".padStart(3, "0");
+```
+
+## Numbers
+
+JavaScript uses 64-bit floating-point numbers.
+
+```js
+const a = 10;
+const b = 3;
+
+a + b;
+a - b;
+a * b;
+a / b;
+a % b;
+a ** b;
+```
+
+Math utilities:
+
+```js
+Math.floor(3.9);
+Math.ceil(3.1);
+Math.round(3.5);
+Math.abs(-5);
+Math.min(1, 2, 3);
+Math.max(1, 2, 3);
+Math.sqrt(16);
+Math.pow(2, 10);
+Math.random();
+```
+
+Conversions and checks:
+
+```js
+Number("42");
+Number("abc"); // NaN
+parseInt("42px");
+parseFloat("3.14");
+String(42);
+(42).toString();
+
+Number.isNaN(NaN);
+Number.isFinite(Infinity);
+Number.isInteger(4.0);
+```
+
+## Booleans and Comparison
+
+Use strict equality and inequality. Avoid `==` and `!=`.
+
+```js
+true && false;
+true || false;
+!true;
+
+1 === 1; // true
+1 === "1"; // false
+1 == "1"; // true, avoid this
+```
+
+Falsy values are `false`, `0`, `""`, `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN`.
+Everything else is truthy, including empty arrays and empty objects.
+
+## Arrow Functions
+
+Arrow functions are the only function syntax used in the course.
+
+```js
+const greet = () => "Hello!";
+const double = (n) => n * 2;
+const add = (a, b) => a + b;
+
+const clamp = (value, min, max) => {
+  if (value < min) return min;
+  if (value > max) return max;
+  return value;
+};
+
+const makePoint = (x, y) => ({ x, y });
+```
+
+Default parameters:
+
+```js
+const connect = (host = "localhost", port = 3000) => `${host}:${port}`;
+```
+
+Async arrow functions:
+
+```js
+const fetchData = async (url) => {
+  const response = await fetch(url);
+  return response.json();
+};
+```
+
+Arrow functions do not have their own `this`; in this course, object methods
+reference the object by name.
+
+## Arrays
+
+Arrays are ordered and zero-indexed.
+
+```js
+const fruits = ["apple", "banana", "cherry"];
+
+fruits[0];
+fruits.length;
+fruits[fruits.length - 1];
+```
+
+Mutating methods:
+
+```js
+const a = [1, 2, 3];
+
+a.push(4);
+a.pop();
+a.unshift(0);
+a.shift();
+a.splice(1, 1);
+a.reverse();
+a.sort();
+```
+
+Non-mutating methods:
+
+```js
+const nums = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6];
+
+nums.slice(2, 5);
+nums.concat([7, 8]);
+nums.includes(4);
+nums.indexOf(1);
+nums.join(", ");
+[1, [2, [3]]].flat();
+[1, [2, [3]]].flat(Infinity);
+```
+
+Sorting numbers:
+
+```js
+const sorted = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5].sort((a, b) => a - b);
+```
+
+## Functional Array Methods
+
+These are the primary iteration tools in this course.
+
+`.map()` transforms every element:
+
+```js
+const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4];
+const doubled = numbers.map((n) => n * 2);
+const labels = numbers.map((n) => `item-${n}`);
+```
+
+`.filter()` keeps matching elements:
+
+```js
+const evens = numbers.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0);
+const big = numbers.filter((n) => n > 3);
+```
+
+`.reduce()` accumulates one value:
+
+```js
+const sum = numbers.reduce((acc, n) => acc + n, 0);
+const max = numbers.reduce((acc, n) => (n > acc ? n : acc), -Infinity);
+```
+
+Build an object from an array:
+
+```js
+const words = ["cat", "dog", "cat", "bird", "dog", "dog"];
+
+const counts = words.reduce((acc, word) => {
+  acc[word] = (acc[word] ?? 0) + 1;
+  return acc;
+}, {});
+```
+
+Find values:
+
+```js
+const users = [
+  { id: 1, name: "Alice" },
+  { id: 2, name: "Bob" },
+];
+
+users.find((u) => u.id === 2);
+users.findIndex((u) => u.id === 2);
+```
+
+Booleans:
+
+```js
+const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
+
+nums.some((n) => n > 4);
+nums.every((n) => n > 0);
+```
+
+Side effects:
+
+```js
+[1, 2, 3].forEach((n) => console.log(n));
+```
+
+Prefer `.map()` when you need returned results.
+
+`.flatMap()` maps then flattens one level:
+
+```js
+const sentences = ["hello world", "foo bar"];
+const words = sentences.flatMap((s) => s.split(" "));
+```
+
+## Objects
+
+Objects are key/value pairs.
+
+```js
+const server = {
+  host: "localhost",
+  port: 3000,
+  tls: false,
+};
+
+server.host;
+server["port"];
+```
+
+Shorthand and computed property names:
+
+```js
+const host = "localhost";
+const port = 3000;
+const config = { host, port };
