Add this to the knowledge base Unit 6: The Sidecar Pattern: Extending Services Without Changing Them
Introduction
Chapter 3 of the textbook introduces the sidecar as the first named single-node pattern. Chapter 1 explains why patterns matter: they give practitioners a shared vocabulary for describing arrangements that would otherwise have to be described from scratch every time. The sidecar solves a problem that appears as soon as a service acquires a second job: a process that serves HTTP and also syncs content has two reasons to change, two teams that might need to edit it, and two separate failure modes to worry about at once. The pattern's answer is a second container, attached to the first, that handles the second job without either container needing to know much about the other.

Unit 5 introduced Docker Compose as the tool for declaring and running multi-container systems. This unit uses that foundation to implement the sidecar: two containers sharing a named volume, where one serves and one syncs, and neither calls the other directly. It is the first unit where the unit of execution is a whole container rather than a function call, and the first where the coordination mechanism is a shared volume rather than a shared variable.

Before You Start
Read: Designing Distributed Systems, Ch. 3 (Sidecar Pattern)
References
Node.js fs module — https://nodejs.org/api/fs.htmlLinks to an external site.
setInterval — https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/setIntervalLinks to an external site.
Compose volumes — https://docs.docker.com/reference/compose-file/volumes/Links to an external site.
docker compose restart — https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/restart/Links to an external site.
Notes
The starting problem
Here's a tiny Node.js server that serves a content directory. To keep that content fresh, it also syncs new content into that directory on a timer, right inside the same file.

// app.js
import http from 'node:http';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const CONTENT_DIR = './content';

const syncContent = () => {
  // stands in for pulling the latest content: a git repo, a CMS, an S3 bucket
  fs.writeFileSync(
    path.join(CONTENT_DIR, 'index.html'),
    `<h1>Updated at ${new Date().toISOString()}</h1>`,
  );
};

fs.mkdirSync(CONTENT_DIR, { recursive: true });
syncContent();
setInterval(syncContent, 10000);

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const html = fs.readFileSync(path.join(CONTENT_DIR, 'index.html'));
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
  res.end(html);
}).listen(3000, () => console.log('Serving on port 3000'));
This works fine. But notice that app.js now has two jobs that have nothing to do with each other: serving HTTP requests, and knowing how content gets refreshed. If you wanted to change where content comes from, how often it refreshes, or swap in a completely different sync mechanism, you'd have to edit and redeploy the exact same file that handles requests — even though the request-handling logic never changed.

Extending the example
Split the two jobs into two separate processes that agree on nothing except a shared directory. One serves. One syncs. Neither calls the other directly.

// app.js — serves only
import http from 'node:http';
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const CONTENT_DIR = './content';

http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const html = fs.readFileSync(path.join(CONTENT_DIR, 'index.html'));
  res.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });
  res.end(html);
}).listen(3000, () => console.log('Serving on port 3000'));
// sync.js — syncs only
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const CONTENT_DIR = './content';

const syncContent = () => {
  fs.writeFileSync(
    path.join(CONTENT_DIR, 'index.html'),
    `<h1>Updated at ${new Date().toISOString()}</h1>`,
  );
  console.log('Content synced');
};

fs.mkdirSync(CONTENT_DIR, { recursive: true });
syncContent();
setInterval(syncContent, 10000);
Create a package.json so Node.js treats these as ES modules:

{
  "type": "module"
}
Open two terminals. In the first, run node app.js. In the second, run node sync.js. Open http://localhost:3000 and reload every few seconds. The timestamp keeps updating, exactly like before, but app.js and sync.js never call each other. They cooperate purely by reading and writing the same directory.

Naming the pattern
What you just built is the sidecar pattern: a second process attached to a primary process, sharing a local resource with it so it can add a capability the primary needs without modifying the primary's code. The primary (app.js) has no dependency on the sidecar's implementation. You could replace sync.js with something that pulls from a git repo, fetches from S3, or syncs on a completely different schedule, and app.js would never need to change.

The sidecar runs as its own independent process, but is deployed and managed together with its primary as one unit. That independence is what makes the pattern work cleanly: app.js only reads the shared file, sync.js only writes it, so there is no shared mutable state being raced over the way there was in Unit 1. The coordination happens through a resource built for exactly this — a shared file — rather than through memory two processes are racing to update.

Exercise
Task: Change the sync interval in sync.js from 10 seconds to 3 seconds, and add a counter that increments on every sync so the page shows both a timestamp and a sync count.

Starting point: The two-file version from the Notes above (the app.js + sync.js split).

You'll know it works when: refreshing http://localhost:3000 shows the sync count climbing roughly every 3 seconds, and you were able to make this entire change inside sync.js without touching app.js at all.

Real World
The Notes version runs both processes on your local machine sharing a directory. In production, the primary service and its sidecar run as separate containers — but the coordination mechanism is exactly the same: a shared volume that both containers can read and write.

Docker Compose lets you define the two containers and their shared volume in a single file. Create Dockerfile:

FROM node:24
WORKDIR /usr/src/app
COPY . .
RUN mkdir -p content
