The sidecar pattern composes: you can attach more than one sidecar to a primary container. Add a second sidecar, monitor.js, that polls index.html every 2 seconds and logs a message whenever the file changes:

// monitor.js
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';

const CONTENT_DIR = './content';
const FILE = path.join(CONTENT_DIR, 'index.html');

let lastContent = '';

setInterval(() => {
  try {
    const current = fs.readFileSync(FILE, 'utf8');
    if (current !== lastContent) {
      console.log(`[monitor] content changed at ${new Date().toISOString()}`);
      lastContent = current;
    }
  } catch {
    // file not yet written
  }
}, 2000);
Add a monitor service to docker-compose.yml that uses the same image, runs node monitor.js, and mounts the same content volume.

You'll know it's working when docker compose logs monitor shows a line every time sync updates the file — and you added this capability without touching app.js or sync.js at all.

